WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.520
I hate to have to start this way today after
the Thanksgiving holiday.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:06.521
But if you're watching on YouTube at least
wait, please don't hit [inaudible]

00:00:06.521 --> 00:00:08.500
the dislike button just yet.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.180
Consider watching this video first.

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:12.320
Before you do that.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:17.770
I am here to talk about the unfortunate reality
that a national Medicare for all is not happening

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:18.869
anytime soon.

00:00:18.869 --> 00:00:22.770
And I'll explain why and I'm not here as an
opponent of Medicare for all.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:24.300
I'm not someone who wants it to fail.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:29.710
I'm an advocate of Medicare for all, but I'm
also an advocate of being realistic with you

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:31.460
if we want to make things happen.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:37.220
And the reality is that without me attacking
Medicare for all, without me attacking the

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:43.480
concept of Medicare for all, I recognize that
national Medicare for all isn't happening

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:44.810
anytime soon.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:49.740
I'm not here to say we should advocate for
centrist policies that are more realistic,

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:52.660
that we should advocate for the policies we
think are the right ones.

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:55.820
And the right progressive policy is Medicare
for all.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:58.890
But I'm not here to tell you unrealistic things.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:02.150
Like if Bernie gets elected, we'll have Medicare
for all in four years.

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:03.150
We won't.

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:04.190
And I'll explain why in a second.

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:08.580
Our politicians do enough of the unrealistic
stuff.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:14.420
I'm here to discuss the realities and I'm
here to tell you today that because national

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:19.190
Medicare for all simply isn't happening anytime
soon, even if the next president supports

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:20.190
it.

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:24.920
We need to be smart, so let's go through this
piece by piece and once we're done, rather

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:29.570
than, you know, calling me a shill or whatever,
tell me where you think I'm wrong on the reasoning

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:31.050
because that's really what this is about.

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:33.730
It's about talking about the facts and reality.

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:38.410
Number one, I've already talked about some
of the areas of concern around Medicare for

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:41.670
all plans, but that's not really what this
segment is about.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:42.670
Yes.

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:48.479
I've talked before about how most of the plans
for how to pay for Medicare for all are unrealistically

00:01:48.479 --> 00:01:52.250
optimistic about how much healthcare spending
per person will go down.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:54.060
If we switch to Medicare for all.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.140
In reality, it could take 10 or even 20 years
to see those costs reductions.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:01:59.729
I've talked about that before.

00:01:59.729 --> 00:02:02.330
That's not what I'm talking about today.

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:04.690
There are political considerations.

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:09.119
Even if Democrats take the Senate in 2020
which would be by the slimmest of margins

00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:13.810
as I've outlined for you before, it's not
going to be enough to pass Medicare for all

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:19.760
because number one, even if Democrats do take
the Senate by a few seats in 2020 you need

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:25.070
60 votes in order to pass something like Medicare
for all which Democrats are not going to have

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:31.150
after this upcoming election, and not all
Democrats even support Medicare for all in

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:32.150
the Senate.

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:36.990
You can then look at 2022 but then if you're
only starting the conversation again, you

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:40.370
still need 60 votes in the Senate to pass
this.

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:45.430
It's a question Mark whether Democrats could
even get a 60 vote majority in 2022 and of

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:49.570
course the alternative would be get rid of
that 60 vote requirement.

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:54.280
You can do that with a one seat majority,
but even Bernie Sanders has hesitated to say

00:02:54.280 --> 00:03:00.960
we should get rid of that a 60 vote requirement
because it could massively backfire if Republicans

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.790
take control again when Republicans take control
again.

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:05.570
So that's a serious political obstacle.

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:09.910
It doesn't matter whether Bernie's president
or Warren's president or whoever, it's just

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:14.489
a real obstacle and then imagine that you
solve that problem.

00:03:14.489 --> 00:03:16.739
You have some bigger issues to deal with.

00:03:16.739 --> 00:03:19.750
We have a conservative Supreme court.

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:23.650
We are going to have a conservative Supreme
court for some time.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:24.860
Why does that matter?

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:30.379
When we talk about Medicare for all two different
reasons, number one, if you don't make private

00:03:30.379 --> 00:03:37.660
insurance illegal as some want to do, it significantly
hurts the financial viability of Medicare

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:38.660
for all.

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:44.700
Making private insurance illegal would be
almost impossible under the current Supreme

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:46.819
court or any Supreme court that's like it.

00:03:46.819 --> 00:03:52.060
It will be fought endlessly by moneyed interests
up to the Supreme court.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:55.269
This is not a Supreme court that will say
private insurance is now illegal.

00:03:55.269 --> 00:03:59.480
It's just not going to happen and that is
something that will hurt the financial viability

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:03.240
of Medicare for all and realistically will
make it not happen politically.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:09.989
Issue number two, you aren't going to be able
to force doctors to accept Medicare for all.

00:04:09.989 --> 00:04:14.769
Many doctors will choose to accept private
insurance because again, it's not going to

00:04:14.769 --> 00:04:19.620
be illegal and you will see more of a move
to what's called self pay.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:23.490
We already see self pay more and more in the
mental health field.

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:28.199
We see it in dentistry because dental insurance
is terrible in the United States.

00:04:28.199 --> 00:04:32.430
We could spend much longer on this Supreme
Supreme court issue, but again there is no

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:37.759
way that a Supreme court that is even remotely
like the one we have right now would rule.

00:04:37.759 --> 00:04:40.419
Doctors have to accept Medicare for all.

00:04:40.419 --> 00:04:45.080
That will be a major problem for the financial
viability of Medicare for all, but it won't

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:50.509
even get to that because it is not going to
be something that you will get 60 votes in

00:04:50.509 --> 00:04:52.539
the Senate for under these parameters.

00:04:52.539 --> 00:04:57.949
So what I want to talk about is not that I
think Medicare for all is bad.

00:04:57.949 --> 00:04:58.949
I think it's good.

00:04:58.949 --> 00:04:59.949
I like it.

00:04:59.949 --> 00:05:05.479
I do see it as a drastic and maybe the best
improvement over the system that we have.

00:05:05.479 --> 00:05:10.080
I don't want to suggest to you that it doesn't
matter if a candidate supports Medicare for

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:11.080
all it does.

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:12.080
We should.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:16.139
We should support candidates who see Medicare
for all as the gold standard and who see it

00:05:16.139 --> 00:05:21.879
as the goal, but you and I have to be realistic
that it's not happening anytime soon.

00:05:21.879 --> 00:05:26.949
So before we even talk about candidates, let's
talk about what could happen.

00:05:26.949 --> 00:05:33.229
Number one, it's important to get some wins
that demonstrate that conceptually Medicare

00:05:33.229 --> 00:05:34.449
for all or something.

00:05:34.449 --> 00:05:35.629
It would work.

00:05:35.629 --> 00:05:38.509
These include, and this, this is so lame.

00:05:38.509 --> 00:05:41.599
I'm almost like going to throw up in my mouth
a little, a little bit saying it.

00:05:41.599 --> 00:05:46.969
We need to support some version of a public
option as an eventual point that we get to.

00:05:46.969 --> 00:05:51.839
I said during Obamacare, Barack Obama should've
started the negotiation for Medicare for all

00:05:51.839 --> 00:05:54.620
so that at minimum we end up with a strong
public option.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:55.620
We didn't.

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:58.689
He started with the public option and we ended
up with just a massive handout of tens of

00:05:58.689 --> 00:06:01.529
millions of new customers to for-profit insurance
companies.

00:06:01.529 --> 00:06:05.839
I know it's so lame and milk toast to talk
about a public option.

00:06:05.839 --> 00:06:12.159
And as I said before, all the public option
will do is estimate, say it'll reduce the

00:06:12.159 --> 00:06:16.839
cost of private insurance by seven to 9% because
of its impact on the market.

00:06:16.839 --> 00:06:23.629
But if we end up with the best possible version
of a public option, ideally it will work well

00:06:23.629 --> 00:06:26.229
because it will be properly set up and funded.

00:06:26.229 --> 00:06:28.849
We can then point to it and say, look, that
was an improvement.

00:06:28.849 --> 00:06:30.180
Let's keep going to the next step.

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:31.180
That's number one.

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:32.180
But that's not all.

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:37.689
Number two, it might be possible to make a
change to the Medicare eligibility age, which

00:06:37.689 --> 00:06:39.229
won't totally rock the boat.

00:06:39.229 --> 00:06:43.699
Like for example, lowering the age to 60 or
maybe to 58.

00:06:43.699 --> 00:06:48.999
This is actually step one in some people's
plans for Medicare, for all Elizabeth Warren

00:06:48.999 --> 00:06:54.389
for example, it talks about let's lower the
age eligibility, the eligibility age, better

00:06:54.389 --> 00:07:00.770
said in steps and uh, that might actually
be a viable first step.

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:04.909
There would probably have to be some kind
of trade though because again, even many Democrats

00:07:04.909 --> 00:07:07.319
in the Senate don't support Medicare for all.

00:07:07.319 --> 00:07:12.210
That's not going to change in 2020 and it's
unlikely to change in 2022 third and this

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:14.689
is to me the most exciting.

00:07:14.689 --> 00:07:16.710
What about state single-payer?

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:21.000
Vermont wanted to do this, but it ended up
failing for a variety of reasons that we could

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:22.819
explore in a different segment.

00:07:22.819 --> 00:07:28.490
If Medicare for all succeeds at the state
level, a serious conversation about national

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:33.999
Medicare for all would hopefully be able to
start Obamacare mirrored years later.

00:07:33.999 --> 00:07:38.520
In many ways, the Romney care system that
started here in Massachusetts, it falls way

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:45.939
short of being what we want, but the point
is state level success can actually push nationally

00:07:45.939 --> 00:07:47.449
implementing some of these policies.

00:07:47.449 --> 00:07:49.180
So please don't kill the messenger.

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:51.479
This is not me attacking Medicare for all.

00:07:51.479 --> 00:07:52.639
I like Medicare for all.

00:07:52.639 --> 00:07:55.970
I want Medicare for all this is the reality.

00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:59.879
So that doesn't mean that we now say, Hey,
you know what?

00:07:59.879 --> 00:08:04.039
If either way, the best we can get as a public
option or low lowering the eligibility age

00:08:04.039 --> 00:08:07.259
for Medicare a few years, you might as well
vote for Joe Biden.

00:08:07.259 --> 00:08:10.059
No, that's not what I'm saying at all.

00:08:10.059 --> 00:08:15.059
What I am saying is that there are a lot of
people out there and they're emailing me regularly

00:08:15.059 --> 00:08:20.900
who seem to be making arguments and decisions
based on the idea that if Bernie wins, we

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:23.050
get Medicare for all in four years.

00:08:23.050 --> 00:08:27.199
That if Warren wins for the people that believe
she's for Medicare for all, and she does seem

00:08:27.199 --> 00:08:30.959
to be, um, uh, then we get it within four
years.

00:08:30.959 --> 00:08:33.660
We just don't, and I'm not being defeatist.

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:36.820
Let's strategize around what we can do.

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:38.040
Let's make it happen.

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:41.970
And if you disagree with me, tell me where
I'm wrong on the facts.

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:43.599
Not that I'm a shill or whatever.

00:08:43.599 --> 00:09:30.770
I want Medicare

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:58.190
for all, but

00:09:58.190 --> 00:10:46.209
I need to be realistic about how we 
get there.

